8869"}},{"id":"7771","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"1708","description":"Numbers put-in: Rich Hoffman, Pete \r\nSkinner and Chuck Estes standing in \r\nfront of the sign acknowledging \r\nAmerican Whitewater's role securing \r\naccess to the Numbers on the \r\nArkansas in Colorado.","caption":"Numbers Put-in","poi_name":"","subject":"Rich Hoffman, Pete Skinner, Chuck Estes","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7771","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7771"}},{"id":"15204","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"5660","description":"At each put-in and take-out there is an \"Attention Boaters\" sign that will give you a variety of information and the current level of the Arkansas River.","caption":"(RM) Attention Boaters Sign","poi_name":"","subject":"Attention Boaters Sign","author":"Rob Maxwell","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/15204","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/15204"}},{"id":"RlaV6nr0IQeynu4o4eDA3","ext":"jpg","post_id":"0a1Cn3kHA8IgNf9yNrsRM","description":null,"caption":"Arkansas @02. The Numbers","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"ppodgorski","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-06-22T22:19:52.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/RlaV6nr0IQeynu4o4eDA3","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/RlaV6nr0IQeynu4o4eDA3"}},{"id":"fbHmwurxB6xI8I1Bonkq6","ext":"jpg","post_id":"0zw0HzDoYxTJ6hwJOkKLf","description":null,"caption":"Arkansas @02. The Numbers","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"ppodgorski","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-06-22T23:32:12.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/fbHmwurxB6xI8I1Bonkq6","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/fbHmwurxB6xI8I1Bonkq6"}},{"id":"32TFV5TT6j0bbJIlxVrbl","ext":"jpg","post_id":"rbtIFyNETIlCB3b8xz5A3","description":null,"caption":"Arkansas @02. The Numbers","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"ppodgorski","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-06-23T15:45:23.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/32TFV5TT6j0bbJIlxVrbl","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/32TFV5TT6j0bbJIlxVrbl"}},{"id":"7770","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"1708","description":"Numbers put-in: Ken Ransford, Rich \r\nHoffman, Pete Skinner and Chuck \r\nEstes celebrating the new access to \r\nthe Numbers on the Arkansas in \r\nColorado acquired through American \r\nWhitewater's access program.\r\n","caption":"Numbers Put-in","poi_name":"","subject":"Ken Ransford, Rich Hoffman, Pete Skinner, Chuck Estes","author":"","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/7770","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/7770"}}],"detail":{"id":"356","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"The Numbers is widely considered just about the best Class IV run in the state of Colorado. At low levels, the river channelizes and is fun IV- down to about 300cfs for playboaters. At about 1000cfs the rapids graduate to IV proper, and over 2200cfs they take on a IV+ character.\n\nThere are six major Class IV rapids creatively named number 1 through 6, although most takeout shortly after #5. Rapid #1 is the most technical, but #4 and #5 generally have the most carnage. Above 3000cfs, the run takes on a big water character, massive holes abound. At these levels, a powerful 8-foot surfing wave forms at the base of rapid #1.5.\n\nMuch of the land on either side of the river is private property, so scouting is most likely illegal. Do what you have to do to be safe. As noted above, most people take out just after #5 where there is an abundance of unofficial takeouts. If the water level is medium to high, consider making the most of a short run and takeout below #6 at the railroad trestle.\n\n**Elevations:** The river at the launch area is about 8,640 feet, while the take out at the railroad trestle is about 8,247 feet.\n\n**Other Information Sources:**\n\n_Colorado Rivers and Creeks II_, by Banks and Eckardt _(The Bible)_\n\nSee also\n\n[Pinecreek](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/355//) (Class IV/V),\n\n[The Numbers](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/356/) (Class IV),\n\n[Fractions/ Frogrock/ Milkrun](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/357/) (Class III),\n\n[Buena Vista](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/4347/) (Class III),\n\n[Browns Canyon](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/358/) (Class III),\n\n[Salida](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/4348/) (Class III),\n\n[Rincon](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/4349/) (Class III),\n\n[Pinnacle Rock](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/359/) (Class III),\n\n[Royal Gorge](http://www.americanwhitewater.org/rivers/id/360/) (Class III/IV).","averageGradient":"71","maxGradient":null,"length":"6.4","correlations":[{"reachID":"356","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"cfs","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":"III","highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":"IVplus","beginLowRunnable":"300","beginMediumRunnable":"1000","beginHighRunnable":"2200","endHighRunnable":"3500"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"ARKANSAS RIVER BELOW GRANITE, CO","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"07087050","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"233","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"11.32","dateTime":"2026-05-12T03:00:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"38.97693889","longitude":"-106.2139833"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sour